====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|tack}} A [[seat#Noun|seat]] for a [[rider]], typically [[make#Verb|made]] of [[leather#Noun|leather]] and [[raise#Verb|raised]] in the [[front#Noun|front]] and [[rear#Noun|rear]], [[place#Verb|placed]] on the [[back#Noun|back]] of a [[horse#Noun|horse]] or other [[animal]], and [[secure#Verb|secured]] by a [[strap#Noun|strap]] around the animal's [[body#Noun|body]].
#* {{RQ:Herbert Travaile|chapter=The Death of Sir {{w|Dodmore Cotton}} Ambassadour, at Cazbeen|page=126|passage=His [Cotton's] Horſe vvas led before, vvith a mourning Veluet '''Saddle''' on his backe, his Coffin had a Crimſon Sattin Quilt lined with purple Silke, ouer him vvas laid his Bible, Svvord and Hat: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Evelyn Diary|volume=I|entrydate=11 June 1652|page=266|passage=My horse's bridle they [robbers] slipt, and search'd y{{sup|e}} '''saddle''', which they pull'd off, but let the horse graze, and then turning againe bridl'd him and tied him to a tree, yet so as he might graze, and thus left me bound.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Directions to Servants|chapter=V (Directions to the Groom)|page=152|passage=Consider your master's health, and rather than let him take long journeys, {{...}} leave one of his horse's fore shoes loose in the morning; or contrive that the '''saddle''' may pinch the beast in the withers; or keep him without corn all night and morning, so that he may tire on the road; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Sewell Black Beauty|chapter=An Old War Horse|page=164|passage=I saw many brave men cut down, many fall mortally wounded from their '''saddles'''.}}
#* {{RQ:T. E. Lawrence Seven Pillars|chapter=X|page=76|passage=So he made the best of it, and prepared for me his own splendid riding-camel, saddled with his own '''saddle''', and hung with luxurious housings and cushions of Nejd leather-work pierced and inlaid in various colours, with plaited fringes and nets embroidered with metal tissues.}}
## A [[similar#Adjective|similar]] [[implement#Noun|implement]] [[use#Verb|used]] to secure [[good#Noun|goods]] to animals; a [[packsaddle]].
## {{synonym of|en|harness saddle|t=the [[part#Noun|part]] of a [[harness#Noun|harness]] which [[support#Verb|supports]] the [[weight#Noun|weight]] of [[pole#Noun|poles]] or [[shaft#Noun|shafts]] [[attach]]ing a [[vehicle]] to a horse or other animal}}
## A [[cushion#Noun|cushion]] used as a seat in a [[cart#Noun|cart]] or other vehicle.
## The [[immovable#Adjective|immovable]] seat of a [[bicycle#Noun|bicycle]], [[motorcycle#Noun|motorcycle]], or similar vehicle.
## {{lb|en|by extension}}
### ''Chiefly preceded by'' '''[[the#Article|the]]''': [[horseriding|horse-riding]] as an [[activity]] or [[occupation]].
###* {{RQ:Ascham Toxophilus|page=212|passage=[A] good horſeman, {{...}} is ſkilfull to knovv, and hable to tell others, hovv, by certain ſure ſignes, a man may choiſe a colte, that is like to prove an other day excellent for the '''ſaddle'''.}}
###* {{RQ:Topsell Foure-footed Beastes|chapter=Of the Camell Dromedarie|page=99|passage=[A]nd the third ſort [of camel] are called ''Ragnahil'', vvhich are of lovver ſtature and learner bodies then the reſidue, vnfit for burthen, and therefore are vſed for the '''ſaddle''', by all the Noble men of ''Numidia'', ''Arabia'', and ''Libia'': {{...}}|footer=Applied to camel-riding.}}
###* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton Rienzi|volume=I|chapter=Looking after the Halter when the Mare is Stolen|page=285|passage=Well said!—are thy friends ripe for the '''saddle'''?}}
###* {{RQ:Irving Rocky Mountains|volume=I|chapter=VI|page=74|passage=He travelled in company with them until they reached the Sweet Water; then taking a couple of horses, one for the '''saddle''', and the other as a packhorse, he started off express for Pierre's Hole, to make arrangements against their arrival, that he might commence his hunting campaign before the rival company.}}
###* {{RQ:Tennyson Idylls|page=152|passage='Sir King, mine ancient wound is hardly whole, / And lets me from the '''saddle''';' and the King / Glanced first at him, then her, and went his way.}}
### {{synonym of|en|saddle brown|t=a [[medium#Adjective|medium]] [[brown#Adjective|brown]] [[colour#Noun|colour]], like that of saddle leather}}
###: {{color panel|634739}}
# Something [[resemble|resembling]] a saddle ''(sense 1)'' in [[appearance]] or [[shape#Noun|shape]].
## A [[low#Adjective|low]] [[point#Noun|point]], in the shape of a saddle, between [[two]] [[hill#Noun|hills]].
##* {{RQ:Dampier New Voyage|chapter=IX|page=267|passage=The Hill ''Zeliſco'' bore S.E. vvhich is a very high Hill in the Country, vvith a '''Saddle''' or bending on the top.}}
##* {{RQ:T. E. Lawrence Seven Pillars|chapter=XXXI|page=180|passage=[W]e had to dismount and lead our animals [camels] up a narrow hill-path with broken steps of rock so polished by long years of passing feet that they were dangerous in wet weather. {{...}} After fifteen minutes of this we were glad to reach a high '''saddle''' on which former travellers had piled little cairns of commemoration and thankfulness.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Voyageur [pseudonym]|title=The Mountain Railways of the Bernese Oberland|magazine=[[w:Modern Railways|Trains Illustrated]]|location=London|publisher={{w|Ian Allan Publishing}}|month=December|year=1960|page=752|issn=0141-9935|oclc=35845948|passage=So we continue climbing to the '''saddle''' of the Kleine Scheidegg, where ahead there comes into view the wide expanse of the Grindelwald valley, backed by the snowy crown of the Wetterhorn.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|John Le Carré}} [pseudonym; David John Moore Cornwell]|chapter=Nelson|title={{w|The Honourable Schoolboy}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hodder & Stoughton|Hodder and Stoughton]]|year=1977|page=514|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/honourableschool0000leca_a6f3/page/514/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-340-22042-9|passage=With Lizzie leading, they scrambled quickly over several false peaks towards the '''saddle'''.}}
## A [[cut#Noun|cut]] of [[meat#Noun|meat]] that [[include]]s both [[loin]]s and part of the [[backbone]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Margaret Dods [pseudonym; {{w|Christian Isobel Johnstone}}]|chapter=Roasting|title=The Cook and Housewife’s Manual.{{nb...|A Practical System of Modern Domestic Cookery and Family Management; Containing a Compendium of French Cookery, and of Fashionable Confectionary, Preparations for Invalids and Convalescents, a Selection of Cheap Dishes, and Numerous Useful Miscellaneous Receipts in the Various Branches of Domestic Economy. [...] To which is Added a Comprehensive Treatise on Domestic Brewing. Eighth Edition. Carefully Revised, and Greatly Enlarged.}}|edition=8th|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:Oliver and Boyd|Oliver & Boyd]]; London; [[w:Simpkin & Marshall|Simpkin, Marshall, & Co.]]|year=1847|section=paragraph 19|pages=106–107|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5R4EAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA106|oclc=25486034|passage=To roast a leg, haunch, or '''saddle''' of mutton. {{...}} A modern refinement is to put ''laver'' in the dripping-pan, which, in basting, imparts a high ''gout''; or a large '''''saddle''''' may be served over a pound and a half of ''laver'', stewed in brown sauce with catsup and seasonings.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Anthony Burgess|authorlink=Anthony Burgess|chapter=6|title=The Enemy in the Blanket|series={{w|The Malayan Trilogy}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Heinemann (publisher)|William Heinemann]]|year=1958|year_published=1979|page=71|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/isbn_434098132/page/71/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-434-09813-2|passage=Certainly, in the gravy soups, turbot, hare, roast '''saddles''', cabinet puddings, boiled eggs at tea-time and bread and butter and meat paste with the morning tray, one tasted one's own decadence: a tradition had been preserved in order to humiliate. Perhaps it really was time the British limped out of Malaya.}}
## {{lb|en|construction}}
### A [[small#Adjective|small]] [[sloped#Adjective|sloped]] or [[tapered#Adjective|tapered]] [[structure#Noun|structure]] that [[help#Verb|helps]] [[channel#Verb|channel]] [[surface water]] to [[drain#Noun|drains]].
### The [[raised#Adjective|raised]] [[floorboard]] in a [[doorway]].
## {{lb|en|dentistry}} The part of a [[denture]] which [[hold#Verb|holds]] the [[artificial]] [[tooth#Noun|teeth]].
## {{lb|en|engineering}} An [[equipment]] part, such as a [[flange#Noun|flange]], which is [[hollow out|hollowed out]] to [[fit#Verb|fit]] upon a [[convex#Adjective|convex]] [[surface#Noun|surface]] and [[serve#Verb|serve]] as a [[means#Noun|means]] of [[attachment]] or [[support#Noun|support]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=November and December|title=Modified G.W.R. "Hall" Class Locomotives|journal=Railway Magazine|page=350|text={{...}} with this arrangement the cylinders are cast separately from the '''saddle''', and bolted to the frames on each side with a fabricated stiffener between the frames. This stiffener is carried up to form the '''saddle''' for the smokebox, and within it is fitted the exhaust pipes from the cylinders to the blast pipe.}}
## {{lb|en|geology}} An {{l|en|anticline|t=[[fold#Noun|fold]] with [[stratum|strata]] [[slop#Verb|sloping]] [[downwards]] on each [[side#Noun|side]]}}; specifically, a [[depression]] [[locate]]d along the [[axial#Adjective|axial]] [[trend#Noun|trend]] of such a fold.
### {{lb|en|chiefly|Australia|mining}} {{synonym of|en|saddle reef|t=a saddle-[[shaped#Adjective|shaped]] [[bed#Verb|bedded]] [[mineral#Noun|mineral]] (usually [[gold-bearing]] [[quartz]]) [[vein#Noun|vein]] [[occur]]ring along the [[crest#Noun|crest]] of an anticline or {{lb|en|less common}} a [[syncline]] (an [[inverted#Adjective|inverted]] saddle)}}
## {{lb|en|geometry}} {{synonym of|en|saddle point|t=a point in the [[range#Noun|range]] of a [[smooth#Adjective|smooth]] [[function#Noun|function]], every [[neighbourhood]] of which [[contain]]s points on each side of its [[tangent#Adjective|tangent]] [[plane#Noun|plane]]}}
## {{lb|en|lutherie}}
### The part of a [[guitar#Noun|guitar]] which supports the [[string#Noun|strings]] and, in an [[acoustic guitar]], [[transfer#Verb|transfers]] their [[vibration]]s through the [[bridge#Noun|bridge]] to the [[soundboard]].
### A small [[object#Noun|object]] ([[traditionally]] made of [[ebony]]) at the bottom of a [[string instrument]] such as a [[cello]], [[viola]], or [[violin]] below the [[tailpiece]] on which the {{l|en|tailgut|t=[[cord#Noun|cord]] securing the tailpiece to the [[instrument]]}} [[rest#Verb|rests]].
## {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[block#Noun|block]] of [[wood#Noun|wood]] with [[concave#Adjective|concave]] depressions at the [[top#Noun|top]] and [[bottom#Noun|bottom]], usually [[fasten]]ed to one [[spar#Noun|spar]] and [[shape#Verb|shaped]] to [[receive#Verb|receive]] the [[end#Noun|end]] of another.
## {{lb|en|zoology}}
### The [[clitellum]] of an [[earthworm]] ([[family]] [[Lumbricidae]]).
### The [[lower#Adjective|lower]] part of the back of a [[domestic]] [[fowl#Noun|fowl]], especially a [[male#Adjective|male]] [[bird#Noun|bird]], bearing the [[saddle feather]]s or [[saddle hackle]]s.
### ''In full'' '''saddle marking''' ''or'' '''saddle patch''': a [[saddlelike|saddle-like]] [[marking#Noun|marking]] on an animal, such as one on the back of an [[adult#Adjective|adult]] [[harp seal]] or [[saddleback seal]] (''[[Pagophilus groenlandicus]]''), or any of [[numerous]] such markings on a [[boa constrictor]] (''[[Boa constrictor]]'').
## {{lb|en|originally|and|chiefly|Canada|US}}
### A piece of leather [[stitch#Verb|stitched]] across the [[instep]] of a [[shoe#Noun|shoe]], usually having a [[different#Adjective|different]] [[colour#Noun|colour]] from the [[rest#Noun|rest]] of the shoe.
### {{synonym of|en|saddle oxford}} ''or'' '''[[saddle shoe]]''' (“a shoe, [[resemble|resembling]] an [[oxford]], which has a saddle ''(sense 11.1)''”)
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Judy Blume|authorlink=Judy Blume|chapter=|title={{w|Tales of a Fourth Grade Nothing}}|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fRu1AwAAQBAJ&pg=PT33|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Dutton Children's Books|Dutton Children’s Books]]|year=1972|page=56|isbn=978-0-525-40720-1|passage='Now then&nbsp;… What'll it be, boys?' Mr Berman asked. / My mother answered. 'Brown-and-white '''saddles''' for Fudge and loafers for Peter.'}}

