# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[put#Verb|put]] a [[saddle#Noun|saddle]] ''(noun sense 1)'' on (an [[animal]]).
##* {{RQ:Coverdale Bible|book=Numbers|chapter=22|verse=21|folio=lxvii|column=2|passage=Then roſe {{w|Balaam}} vp in the mornynge, & '''ſadled''' his Aſſe, & wente w&#874; the prynces of y&#868; Moabites.}}
##* {{RQ:Markham Countrey Contentments|book=1|chapter=Of the Ordering and Dyeting of the Running Horse|page=84|passage='''Saddle''' your ''horſe'' on the race day in the ſtable before you leade him forth, and fixe both the pannell and the girthes to his backe and ſides vvith Shooe-makers vvaxe to preuent all daungers.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard Percivale|authorlink=Richard Percivale|author2=John Minsheu|authorlink2=John Minsheu|entry=Báyo|title=A Dictionary in Spanish and English:{{nb...|First Published into the English Tongue by Ric. Percivale Gent. Now Enlarged and Amplified with Many Thousand Words, as by This Marke * to Each of Them Prefixed may Appeare; together with the Accenting of Euery Word thorowout the Whole Dictionarie, for the True Pronunciation of the Language, as also for the Diuers Signification of One and the Selfsame Word: And for the Learners Ease and Furtherance, the Declining of All Hard and Irregular Verbs; and for the Same Cause the Former Order of the Alphabet is Altered, Diuers Hard and Uncouth Phrases and Speeches out of Sundry of the Best Authors Explained, with Diuers Necessary Notes and Especiall Directions for All Such as shall be Desirous to Attaine the Perfection of the Spanish Tongue. All Done By Iohn Minsheu Professor of Languages in London. Hereunto for the Further Profit and Pleasure of the Learner or Delighted in This Tongue, is Annexed an Ample English Dictionarie, Alphabetically Set Downe with the Spanish Words thereunto Adioyned, as also an Alphabeticall Table of the Arabicke and Moorish Words now Commonly Receiued and Used in the Spanish Tongue, which Being Dispersed in Their Seuerall Due Places throughout the Whole Dictionarie are marked thus †; by the Same Iohn Minsheu. For the Right Use of This Worke, I Referre You to the Directions before the Dictionarie, Contriued in Diuers Points Differing from Other Dictionaries heretofore Set Forth.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed at London by}} Iohn Haviland for {{w|William Aspley}}|year=1623|page=44|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CDNS9Uk5d-MC&pg=PA44|column=1|oclc=1339104092|passage=vno pienſa el Báyo, otro que lo ensilla, ''the bay horſe thinketh one thing, and he that '''ſaddleth''' him thinketh another thing.''}}
##* {{RQ:Livy Holland Romane Historie|book=XXXVII|page=766|passage=Some fevv of the horſes kept their ſtanding ſtill unaffrighted, and even thoſe they had much ado to '''ſaddle''', to bridle, and to mount upon; {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=R[ichard] W[illiams] Morgan|authorlink=Richard Williams Morgan|chapter=The Venedotian and His Land|title=Raymond de Monthault, the Lord Marcher: A Legend of the Welch Borders.{{nb...|[...] In Three Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Richard Bentley (publisher)|Richard Bentley]],{{nb...|New Burlington Street.}}|year=1853|volume=III|page=50|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ArgBAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA2-PA50|oclc=12118875|passage="Nay, nay, Reynallt," said Ap Teudor, replying to the angry glance of the Forester, "thou '''saddlest''' the wrong steed: like the Abbot, I have no voice but that of obedience."|footer={{small|Used figuratively.}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=George Ferguson|chapter=Canto VI. Earth’s Phases: The Ministry of Night: Early Temples.|title=Our Earth—Night to Twilight|location=London|publisher=[[w:T. Fisher Unwin|T[homas] Fisher Unwin]]{{nb...|Paternoster Square}}|year=1892|volume=I|page=155|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=oWpIAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA155|oclc=560068919|passage=And he &#91;{{w|Abraham}}&#93; doth rise up with the morrow's dawn, / And for that bidden flame, the wood straightway / He cleaveth now; and '''saddleth''' his ass; / And taking him his son &#91;{{w|Isaac}}&#93; and servants hence, / Doth journey thitherward—full of his God.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard Wagner|authorlink=Richard Wagner|chapter=[[w:Sigurd|Siegfried]]’s Death|translator={{w|William Ashton Ellis}}|title=Richard Wagner’s Prose Works|location=London|publisher=[[w:Charles Kegan Paul|Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner & Co.]]|year=1899|volume=VIII (Posthumous, etc.)|section=Act III, scene ii|page=45|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/cu31924022322550/page/n72/mode/1up|oclc=857094865|passage=[[w:Brunhild|Brünnhild]]! Brünnhild! / Thou shining child of [[w:Odin|Wotan]]! / Bright-beaming through the night, / the hero I see thee draw near: / with holy earnest smile / thou '''saddlest''' thy horse, / that dew-dripping / cleaveth the clouds.}}
## To put (something) on to another [[thing]] like a saddle on an animal.
##* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|chapter=Persius|satire=5|lines=207–208|pages=68|passage=Reſolv'd for Sea, the Slaves thy Baggage pack; / Each '''ſaddled''', vvith his Burden on his back: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Audubon Ornithological Biography|chapter=Purple Grackle or Common Crow-blackbird. ''Quiscalus versicolor'', Vieill.{{nb...|Plate VII. Male and Female.}}|page=39|passage=The centre of the nest is what I would call '''''saddled''''' on the bough, the materials being laid so that the nest is thinner in its middle part and thicker at the two opposite sides, so as to have a firm hold.}}
## {{lb|en|figuratively}}
### To [[enter#Verb|enter]] (a [[trained#Adjective|trained]] [[horse#Noun|horse]]) into a [[race#Noun|race]].
### {{lb|en|often|passive}} ''Chiefly followed by'' '''[[with]]''': to [[burden#Verb|burden]] or [[encumber]] (someone) with some [[problem#Noun|problem]] or [[responsibility]].
###: {{ux|en|He has been '''saddled''' with the task of collecting evidence of the theft.}}
###: {{ux|en|They went shopping and left me '''saddled''' with two children to look after.}}
###* {{quote-journal|en|title=Dr. Beeching Previews the Plan for British Railways|journal={{w|Modern Railways}}|location=Shepperton, Surrey|publisher={{w|Ian Allan Publishing}}|month=December|year=1962|page=377|issn=0026-8356|oclc=884584817|passage=They '''saddled''' themselves with the handling of light flows on a multiplicity of branch lines, and they sacrificed the speed, reliability and low cost of through train operation, even over the main arteries of the system.}}
### ''Chiefly followed by'' '''[[on#Preposition|on]]''' ''or'' '''[[upon]]''': to [[place#Verb|place]] (a [[burden#Noun|burden]] or responsibility) or [[thrust#Verb|thrust]] (a problem) on someone.
###* {{RQ:Besant Rice Chaplain|volume=III|chapter=How Lord Chudleigh Received His Freedom|page=248|passage=I did not dislose the name of the man I proposed, because I found her only too eager to marry anyone upon whom she could '''saddle''' her debts, and so make him either pay them or change places with her.}}
### {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[control#Verb|control]] or [[restrain#Verb|restrain]] (someone or something), as if using a saddle; to [[bridle#Verb|bridle]], to [[harness#Verb|harness]], to [[rein in]].
###* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=I|chapter=V|page=567|passage=But he never would believe that Providence had sent a few men into the world ready booted and spurred to ride, and millions ready '''saddled''' and bridled to be ridden.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} To [[get#Verb|get]] (someone) to [[do#Verb|do]] a [[burdensome]] [[talk#Noun|task]].
###* {{RQ:Scott Journal|date=25 October 1826|page=283|passage=Picked up Sotheby, who endeavoured to '''saddle''' me for a review of his polyglot {{w|Virgil}}. I fear I shall scarce convince him that I know nothing of the Latin lingo.}}
## {{lb|en|woodworking}} To [[cut#Verb|cut]] a saddle-[[shaped#Adjective|shaped]] [[notch#Noun|notch]] in (a [[log#Noun|log]] or other [[piece#Noun|piece]] of [[wood#Noun|wood]]) so it can [[fit#Verb|fit]] together with other such logs or pieces; also, to fit (logs or other pieces of wood) together with this [[method#Noun|method]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete}}
### To put something on to (another thing) like a saddle on an animal.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|chiefly|Canada|US}} ''Often followed by'' '''[[up#Preposition|up]]'''.
## To put a saddle on an animal.
##* {{RQ:T. E. Lawrence Seven Pillars|chapter=LXXI|page=394|passage=Next day, while we were '''saddling''', Ali and Abd el Kader appeared.}}
## Of a [[person#Noun|person]]: to [[get#Verb|get]] into a saddle.
##* {{RQ:Burger Rossetti Lenore|page=26|passage=Till the dead midnight we '''saddled''' not,— / I have journeyed far and fast— / And hither I come to carry thee back / Ere the darkness shall be past.}}
##* {{RQ:Kingsley Hereward the Wake|volume=I|chapter=How Hereward was Outlawed, and Went North to Seek His Fortunes|page=61|passage=[A]y, every churl who owns a manor, if he dares—must needs arm and '''saddle''', and levy war on his own behalf, and harry and slay the King's lieges, if he have not garlic to his roast goose every time he chooses {{...}}}}

